-- title:
--   Idempotent cutting out the local factor at a section
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local Noetherian ring that is adically complete for its maximal ideal (in Mathlib's sense: the canonical map to the limit of the quotients by the powers of `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R` is bijective, i.e. Hausdorff and precomplete), let $S$ be a commutative $R$-algebra which is finite as an $R$-module, and let $\varphi \colon S \to R$ be an $R$-algebra homomorphism. The assertion is that there exists an element $e \in S$ such that $e$ is idempotent, $e \cdot e = e$; $\varphi(e) = 0$; and the quotient ring $S / (e)$, where $(e)$ is the ideal generated by the singleton $\{e\}$, is a local ring (in particular nontrivial). No uniqueness, primitivity or further description of $e$ is claimed, and the complementary idempotent $1 - e$ is not mentioned; the conclusion is purely the existence of one such $e$.
--
--   This is the standard splitting off of the connected component of an $R$-point: a finite algebra over a complete local ring decomposes as the product of its local factors at its finitely many maximal ideals, and $1-e$ is the idempotent belonging to the maximal ideal $\varphi^{-1}(\mathfrak m_R)$, so that $S/(e)$ is the local factor through which $\varphi$ factors. It is used in the construction of the connected–étale sequence for a finite flat group scheme over $\mathbb Z_p$, where $\varphi$ is the counit of the coordinate Hopf algebra.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsAdicComplete_exists_isIdempotentElem_apply_eq_zero_isLocalRing_quotient.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
